Beijing University of Posts and TelecommunicationsChina</p>
</div>
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">SpecTokenizer: A Lightweight Streaming Codec in the Compressed Spectrum Domain<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>* Corresponding author.</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zixiang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Guochang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yifeng
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jianqiang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Audio Innovation Technology Department
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Computer Science
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Neural Audio Codecs (NACs) have gained growing attention in recent years as technologies for audio compression and audio representation in speech language models. While mainstream NACs typically require G-level computation and M-level parameters, the performance of lightweight and streaming NACs remains underexplored. This paper proposes SpecTokenizer, a lightweight streaming codec that operates in the compressed spectral domain. Composed solely of alternating CNN and RNN layers, SpecTokenizer achieves greater efficiency and better representational capability through multi-scale modeling in the compressed spectrum domain. At 4 kbps, the proposed SpecTokenizer achieves comparable or superior performance compared to the codec with state-of-the-art lightweight architecture while requiring only 20% of the computation and 10% of the parameters. Furthermore, it significantly outperforms the codec when using similar computational and storage resources.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">keywords</h6>neural audio codec, lightweight, streaming, single codebook, compressed spectrum domain
</div><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_email"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">email: </span>wzx802@bupt.edu.cn, {baron.zhang, nolan.he, alex.wei}@anker-in.com</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Neural Audio Codec (NAC) compresses audio signals into sequences of discrete codes for efficient storage and transmission <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Recently, NAC has gained growing attention as a key technology in Speech Language Modeling (SLM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. By converting continuous audio signals into discrete codes, Large Language Models (LLMs)—which have achieved great success in text processing—can now be applied to various speech processing tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Many high-performance NAC methods have been proposed, optimized for higher audio quality, bitrate efficiency, and lower computational cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. However, most of these models have G-level computational complexity and M-level parameter counts, making them unsuitable for deployment on edge hardware. The performance of models with lower computational and parameter scales has not been thoroughly explored. On the other hand, in signal-level tasks like speech enhancement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, speech signal improvement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and music source separation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, spectrogram-based models often perform better. Yet, in audio codecs, spectrogram-based codecs often underperform compared to waveform-based codecs. To bridge these gaps, we propose SpecTokenizer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When NACs are used for audio reconstruction and as tokenizers in SLMs, the following features are particularly important:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Audio Quality</span>: Critical for NAC performance, impacting users and tasks. NACs should faithfully reconstruct audio details without distortion, vital for applications like speech communication and modeling.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bitrate Efficiency</span>: Refers to transmitting information efficiently per unit of bitrate. A NAC with high bitrate efficiency can achieve comparable or superior audio quality at a lower bitrate.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Streaming</span>: To achieve real-time interaction with simultaneous speaking and responding, codecs need streaming support for low-latency speech encoding and response generation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single Codebook</span>: Single-codebook models are favored over multi-codebook models since the latter introduce additional design complexity to the SLM architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low Computational Complexity</span>: NACs with low computational complexity enable faster encoding and decoding, reducing computational demands and allocating more resources to SLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low Parameter Count</span>: NACs with fewer parameters require less storage, reducing memory demands and freeing up resources for edge hardware.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low Token Rate</span>: Longer sequences can slow down and destabilize LLM training. Therefore, it’s preferable to use NAC models with a low token rate to support SLMs.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduces SpecTokenizer, a lightweight streaming codec in the compressed spectral domain—the first neural audio codec with M-level computation and K-level parameters. Composed solely of alternating CNN and RNN layers, SpecTokenizer achieves efficiency and enhanced representational capability through multi-scale modeling in the compressed spectrum domain. SpecTokenizer offers streaming capability, low computational and memory demands, a wide bitrate range, and a single codebook design, while maintaining high audio quality. Under similar computational and storage resources, SpecTokenizer significantly improves audio quality compared to state-of-the-art codecs. Furthermore, after model miniaturization, SpecTokenizer achieves comparable or superior performance using only 20% of the computation and 10% of the parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, most well-known neural audio codecs have been based on vector quantization (VQ) networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, such as SoundStream <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, EnCodec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, and DAC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Among them, SoundStream is the first universal codec handling multiple types of audio. EnCodec improves compression rates by integrating a lightweight Transformer language model in the discrete latent space. Building on this, researchers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> have explored quantization dropout, a bitrate scalable technique. They have also shown that periodic inductive bias functions outperform common activation functions in audio signal modeling. These models process audio waveforms directly and are therefore categorized as waveform-based codecs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, spectrogram-based codecs focus on processing more intuitive audio spectral features. For example, Lyra <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> converts audio waveforms into log mel-spectrograms and directly quantizes them into tokens. Due to the irreversibility of mel-spectrograms, Lyra relies on a vocoder to synthesize waveforms. To avoid the inefficiency of heavy vocoders, codecs like TFNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> and ESC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> use the invertible Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT) to convert waveforms into complex spectra. This design allows the reconstructed STFT spectra to be losslessly converted back into waveforms via the inverse STFT. FreqCodec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> introduces depthwise and grouped convolutions into the encoder and decoder. This significantly reduces computational and parameter complexity while maintaining speech quality comparable to state-of-the-art codecs at the time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.21209v1/architecture.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/774;" width="476" height="774"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The architecture of the proposed SpecTokenizer.
<span id="S3.F1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DRC</span> denotes dynamic range compression, <span id="S3.F1.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DRE</span> denotes dynamic range expansion,
and <span id="S3.F1.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FLNorm</span> denotes frame-wise layer normalization.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Overview</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Method ‣ SpecTokenizer: A Lightweight Streaming Codec in the Compressed Spectrum Domain" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the architecture of SpecTokenizer. The speech signal <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is transformed into a complex spectrogram <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> via STFT, followed by <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic Range Compression (DRC)</span> to obtain <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. The encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> extracts latent features <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the quantization layer <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> generates the compressed representation <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. The decoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> reconstructs the compressed spectrum <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. After <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic Range Expansion (DRE)</span> to get <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, the inverse STFT (iSTFT) is applied to obtain the reconstructed audio signal <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Spectral Compression</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experience shows that audio signals typically have extremely high dynamic ranges in amplitude, which poses challenges for directly processing raw spectral data. To address this, we perform <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">spectral compression</span> on the spectrum. The core idea is to apply a compression function to the amplitude while preserving the phase.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the dynamic range compression stage, given a complex spectrum <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, we compress it as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{c}=f(|s|)\cdot\operatorname{sign}(s),\quad\text{where }f(|s|)=|s|^{1/p}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{c}=f(|s|)\cdot\operatorname{sign}(s),\quad\text{where }f(|s|)=|s|^{1/p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s|</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the magnitude of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sign}(s)=s/|s|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sign}(s)=s/|s|</annotation></semantics></math> represents its phase, and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is the compression coefficient.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the dynamic range expansion stage, after obtaining the reconstructed compressed spectrum <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> from the model, we apply the inverse function <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to convert the compressed-scale spectrum back into the linear-scale spectrum:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}=f^{-1}(|\hat{s}_{c}|)\cdot\operatorname{sign}(\hat{s}_{c}),\quad\text{where }f^{-1}(|s_{c}|)=|s_{c}|^{p}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}=f^{-1}(|\hat{s}_{c}|)\cdot\operatorname{sign}(\hat{s}_{c}),\quad\text{where }f^{-1}(|s_{c}|)=|s_{c}|^{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Encoder and Decoder</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our model’s encoder and decoder architecture is inspired by MTFAA-Net <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. The encoder <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> starts with a 2D complex convolution, followed by <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> alternating frequency down-sampling blocks (<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fdown</span>) and RNN2D blocks. Each <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fdown</span> block contains a 2D strided convolution with channel number <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, kernel size <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, stride <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and the Snake2D activation function. Each RNN2D block includes a <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">F</span>rame-wise <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">L</span>ayer <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold">Norm</span>alization <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(FLNorm)</span>, a Tanh activation function, a GRU layer, a 2D convolution with kernel size and stride set to 1, and the Snake2D activation function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">RNNs are suitable for tasks with short historical dependencies like audio encoding and decoding, and they have low memory consumption during inference, making them ideal for lightweight edge deployment. The frame-wise layer normalization is specifically designed for RNNs, featuring streaming processing, stable training, and accelerated convergence. It normalizes over the channel and frequency dimensions at each time step:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}=\frac{1}{C\times F}\sum_{c=1}^{C}\sum_{f=1}^{F}Y_{c,t,f}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>F</mi></munderover><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}=\frac{1}{C\times F}\sum_{c=1}^{C}\sum_{f=1}^{F}Y_{c,t,f}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}_{c,t,f}=\frac{Y_{c,t,f}-\mu_{t}}{\sqrt{\sigma^{2}_{t}+\epsilon}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}_{c,t,f}=\frac{Y_{c,t,f}-\mu_{t}}{\sqrt{\sigma^{2}_{t}+\epsilon}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{c,t,f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{c,t,f}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input tensor; <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> are the channel and frequency dimensions; <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are the mean and variance over <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>; and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a small constant to prevent division by zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The Snake2D activation function captures periodic features in the data. The decoder mirrors the encoder’s structure but uses transposed convolutions instead of strided convolutions, with strides opposite to those of the encoder.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Discriminator</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use two discriminators: the Multi-Period Discriminator (MPD) from HiFi-GAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, and the frequency-domain Multi-Band Multi-Scale STFT Discriminator (MBMS-STFT) from DAC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, which improves phase modeling in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Loss Function</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The loss function of SpecTokenizer is written as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{\text{rec}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}+\lambda_{\text{adv}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{adv}}+\lambda_{\text{feat}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{feat}}+\lambda_{\text{cmt}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{cmt}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>feat</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>feat</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>cmt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cmt</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{\text{rec}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}+\lambda_{\text{adv}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{adv}}+\lambda_{\text{feat}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{feat}}+\lambda_{\text{cmt}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{cmt}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{adv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{adv}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{feat}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>feat</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{feat}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{cmt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>cmt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{cmt}}</annotation></semantics></math> are weight coefficients for the reconstruction loss <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>, adversarial loss <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{adv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{adv}}</annotation></semantics></math>, feature matching loss <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{feat}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>feat</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{feat}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and commitment loss <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{cmt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>cmt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{cmt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6 </span>Single Large Codebook</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the single large codebook setting, we initialize the codebook using a clustering method and update it via exponential moving average (EMA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. To improve codebook utilization, we introduce factorized codebook lookup <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and expiration mechanism<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Due to a bug in the widely used EnCodec code, the expiration mechanism has not been effectively used in many codecs. See <a href="https://github.com/facebookresearch/encodec/issues/61" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/facebookresearch/encodec/issues/61</a></span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. Since the codebook size is much larger than the batch data volume—leading to high repetition in batch-sampled data—we add a data pool module. This module accumulates historical data on a larger scale to better represent the data distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of SpecTokenizer and baseline codecs.
</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Codec</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Streaming</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VQ Type</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.5.1.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T1.5.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Domain</span></span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;">SpecTokenizer (Ours)</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;">✓</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;">RVQ / Single</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.5.2.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T1.5.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Compressed Complex</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;">EnCodec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;">✓</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;">RVQ</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.5.3.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S4.T1.5.3.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Waveform</span>
</span></td></tr>
